{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# P1.4 — rebar gc + maintenance doctrine (bounded growth, recovery-safe)\n\n## Context (problem + research)\n\nEvery mutation is a committed file, and the write path sets `gc.auto=0`\n(`_store/event_append.py:64 _ensure_gc_auto_zero`) so a background `git gc` can't\nprune the reflog commits the reset-recovery net relies on (now in\n`rebar._store.sync:54-77`, `reset --hard origin/tickets` for unrelated/diverged\nhistories, \"recoverable via reflog\"). The cost is **unbounded** loose-object/pack\ngrowth on long-lived `tickets` branches. git-bug packs ops into fewer objects with\na cache; Fossil's SQLite sidesteps small-object blowup. rebar sells \"built to\nscale\" but ships no safe reclaim path. Akka's distributed-data pruning teaches the\ndiscipline: gate discard on dissemination + a TTL marker, never wall-clock alone.\n\n## Evidence (experiments)\n\n- EXP6: a discarded commit **survives** `git gc --prune=14.days.ago` but is\n  **destroyed** by `--prune=now` -> a conservative window is mandatory.\n- EXP-R6 (real code): the gc recipe on the **real `.tickets-tracker` orphan-branch\n  worktree** packed 26 -> 0 loose objects and `rebar show` still read correctly.\n\n## What / Implementation detail\n\nOperator-only `rebar gc` (**not** over MCP, like `init`), under the write lock\n(I5) and only when not mid-recovery (reuse\n`rebar._store.lock.check_no_rebase_in_progress` — no leading underscore):\n1. `git -C <tracker> reflog expire --expire=<window> --expire-unreachable=<window> --all`\n   (BOTH flags — EXP6: unreachable objects are governed by `--expire-unreachable`/\n   `--prune`).\n2. `git -C <tracker> gc --prune=<window>.ago` (**never `--prune=now`**); conservative\n   default `gc.reflog_window` = 14 days that must exceed the worst-case offline/\n   un-pushed interval.\n3. Report bytes reclaimed. A `--compact-first` flag runs per-ticket `compact`\n   over eligible tickets first (I1/I9-safe: SNAPSHOT folds, `*.retired` renames\n   union). Document the \"compact then gc\" cadence in `docs/concurrency.md`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] operator-only `rebar gc` (reflog expire --expire+--expire-unreachable, gc --prune=<window>.ago, never now)\n- [ ] `--compact-first` runs per-ticket compaction before packing\n- [ ] mid-recovery guard via `check_no_rebase_in_progress`; not exposed over MCP\n- [ ] gc-then-recover regression test + bytes-reclaimed sanity on a synthetic many-event ticket\n- [ ] `docs/concurrency.md` documents the compact-then-gc cadence and window rationale\n\nSee `docs/remediation-implementation-plan.md` (P1.4).\n- [ ] `rebar gc` reclaims space and reports it; reads work afterward.\n- [ ] A `gc`-then-recover regression proves `_store.sync` recovery still works after gc\n- [ ] Refuses to run mid-recovery; absent from the MCP surface.\n"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785007205320965001,"uuid":"f84e62d1-7679-43c2-8514-0d9954f96c8c"}